<!DOCTYPE html>
<%!-- data-bp-theme (ts-w4e) — the paper's workspace THEME IDENTITY, stamped
      server-side so the reader skin paints on the first byte. It is orthogonal
      to light/dark mode: the reader defaults to prefers-color-scheme, and the
      pre-paint script below stamps data-theme (persisted choice from the
      dark/light pill, or the OS scheme) — the @media blocks stay complete as
      the no-JS fallback, so reader-dark-parity stays green. This attribute
      only selects WHICH theme. Omitted for the default (evergreen) theme, so a
      default paper is byte-identical to before this feature. --%>
<html lang="en" data-bp-theme={bp_theme_attr(assigns)}>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
  <meta name="csrf-token" content={get_csrf_token()} />
  <%!-- Pre-paint mode stamp: synchronous so data-theme is set before first
        paint (no flash of the wrong mode) — the same sanctioned Golden Rule 4
        exception root.html.heex carries: a few lines that set one <html>
        attribute, never a fetched script. Default follows the OS scheme until
        the user clicks the dark/light pill (end of <body>); the choice then
        persists in localStorage under `barkpark_theme` — the SAME key Studio
        uses, so one choice carries across surfaces. While no choice is
        stored, OS scheme changes are mirrored live. The generated
        html[data-theme] companion blocks key off this stamp; the
        prefers-color-scheme blocks remain the no-JS / first-paint fallback. --%>
  <script nonce={assigns[:csp_nonce]}>
    (function () {
      var KEY = "barkpark_theme";
      function osTheme() {
        return window.matchMedia("(prefers-color-scheme: light)").matches ? "light" : "dark";
      }
      var stored = null;
      try { stored = localStorage.getItem(KEY); } catch (e) {}
      document.documentElement.dataset.theme = stored || osTheme();
      // Follow OS live ONLY while the user has made no explicit choice.
      window.matchMedia("(prefers-color-scheme: dark)").addEventListener("change", function (e) {
        try { if (localStorage.getItem(KEY)) return; } catch (err) {}
        document.documentElement.dataset.theme = e.matches ? "dark" : "light";
      });
    })();
  </script>
  <%!-- A7: the reader is a LiveView — <.live_title> keeps the browser tab in
        sync when :page_title changes (the static ternary never updated live).
        The default carries NO site name: live_title appends the suffix to the
        default too, so "Paper · Barkpark" + suffix would double the brand. --%>
  <.live_title default="Paper" suffix=" · Barkpark">{assigns[:page_title]}</.live_title>
  <%!-- Social-share head (preview-contract pc-w2): og/twitter/JSON-LD emitted
        from the paper's preview manifest, IMMEDIATELY after <title> and BEFORE
        the multi-KB inline style blob (Slack reads only the first 32 KB). The
        `:preview` assign is the write-time `content["preview"]` (or the pc-w1
        read-time projection / a degraded core manifest) that BulldocsLive.mount
        and the share-link / scoped-paper controllers set; `assigns[:preview]`
        is nil-safe — a render path that assigns none renders site-default meta,
        never crashes (charter D12). --%>
  <BarkparkWeb.ShareMeta.head preview={assigns[:preview]} page_title={assigns[:page_title]} />
  <%!-- asciinema-player CSS for `asciicast` blocks (a stylesheet, not a blocking
        <script> — the golden no-blocking-script-in-head rule covers JS; the
        player JS itself loads `defer` in the body alongside mermaid). Pinned to
        3.8.0, the version proven in .artifacts/proofs-index.html. --%>
  <link rel="stylesheet" href="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/asciinema-player@3.8.0/dist/bundle/asciinema-player.css" />
  <style>
    *, *::before, *::after { box-sizing: border-box; }
    body { margin: 0; background: #0b0b0d; color: #e7e7ea;
           font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, "Segoe UI", sans-serif; }
    .bp-paper-shell { max-width: 820px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 32px 24px 96px; }

    <%!-- Source Serif 4 — self-hosted variable woff2 (Roman + Italic in
          priv/static/fonts/, served at /fonts/ by Plug.Static). The reader's
          serif prose stack (`font.reading`) names "Source Serif 4" as its
          web-safe fallback, but the reader head shipped ZERO @font-face — so
          off-macOS (no Iowan Old Style / Palatino) it fell through to bare
          `serif` instead of the intended face. These declarations self-host it
          on the reader exactly as root.html.heex does for Studio (S9). They do
          NOT change the stack order: on macOS Iowan still wins; this only makes
          the already-named fallback real. Source Serif 4, SIL OFL 1.1 — see
          fonts/SourceSerif4-OFL.txt. Hand-authored — OUTSIDE the GENERATED
          marker below (nothing here fans from tokens.json). --%>
    @font-face {
      font-family: 'Source Serif 4';
      src: url('/fonts/SourceSerif4Variable-Roman.woff2') format('woff2');
      font-weight: 200 900; font-style: normal; font-display: swap;
    }
    @font-face {
      font-family: 'Source Serif 4';
      src: url('/fonts/SourceSerif4Variable-Italic.woff2') format('woff2');
      font-weight: 200 900; font-style: italic; font-display: swap;
    }

    <%!-- The ONE canonical paper-surface source (paper-surface.css via
          Barkpark.PortableDoc.Render.Stylesheet) — the SAME bytes Studio embeds.
          It provides the `--paper-*`/`--bp-*` tokens + `.bp-paper-surface`
          element rules. It is embedded BEFORE the reader skin below so the
          bp-theme `--paper-*` overrides (later + more specific) win. Surface
          CHROME (page bg/width/padding) is NOT in this source — the reader owns
          its own 820/720px shell above, so nothing here clobbers it. --%>
    <%= raw(Barkpark.PortableDoc.Render.Stylesheet.css()) %>

    /* BEGIN GENERATED: tokens (design/tokens.json — regenerate: node design/emit.mjs --write; do not hand-edit) */
    /* MOTION ladder (tokens.motion) — theme-invariant, so it sits on the
       page root rather than inside any of the reader's palette scopes. The
       reader's own chrome (view toggle, action bar, mail popup) is the
       consumer; a duration does not flip with a skin. */
    :root {
      --dur-1: 0.15s;
      --dur-2: 0.18s;
      --dur-3: 0.28s;
    }
    /* Article chrome — applied only when BulldocsLive marks the doc as
       `content["style"] == "article"` (the LiveView stamps the
       `.bp-paper-article` class on its <main>). Cool bp-theme page, a
       centered ~720px reading column (incl. padding), serif base — so the
       inline-styled article blocks sit on a proper page, not bare white
       full-width. Non-article papers keep the dark chrome above, untouched.

       READER SKIN: token overrides layered over the canonical paper-surface
       source (embedded above). These `--paper-*` values are the historical
       bp-theme reader look. They are set on the <body> AND on the reader
       <main> (`.bp-paper-shell.bp-paper-article`, which now also carries
       `bp-paper-surface`) so they WIN over the shared source's fallback
       `.bp-paper-surface { --paper-* }` block — otherwise render.ex's inline
       `var(--paper-*, hex)` block HTML would resolve to the Studio defaults on
       the reader on the bp-theme (a shared ink #15211d, rule #dde7e2,
       etc.). Dark-mode note: the reader stamps `html[data-theme]` only via
       its pre-paint toggle script (localStorage `barkpark_theme`, shared
       with Studio); before that script runs — and with JS off — the
       `prefers-color-scheme: dark` block below (also carried onto <main>)
       owns reader dark mode, flipping the palette to cool bp-dark with
       light ink so the inline `var(--paper-*)` HTML stays readable. The
       `html[data-theme]` companions further down repeat both palettes so an
       explicit toggle beats the OS scheme. Without the <main>-scoped
       override the shared light fallback would paint dark ink on the
       browser's dark theme — the visibility bug. */
    body:has(.bp-paper-article),
    body:has(.bp-paper-article) .bp-paper-shell.bp-paper-article {
      --paper-bg:         #f6faf9;
      --paper-bg-deep:    #eaf1ee;
      --paper-ink:        #15211d;
      --paper-ink-soft:   #55635e;
      --paper-rule:       #dde7e2;
      --paper-accent:     #1e5347;
      /* Link border-bottom tone (`.bp-paper-surface a` + walk.ex inline links
         read it). Light value matches the shared source's fallback; the dark
         block below MUST re-skin it — otherwise dark-mode link borders resolve
         to this light rgba and vanish against the dark page. */
      --paper-accent-soft: rgba(30, 83, 71, 0.10);
      --paper-reading-accent: #a23925; /* S7 stub — S8 consumes */
    }
    body:has(.bp-paper-article) {
      background: var(--paper-bg-deep);
      color: var(--paper-ink);
      /* THE READING SERIF, on the reader's <body>. This used to be a FOURTH
         hand-kept copy of the stack (paper-surface.css, root.html.heex and
         the paper-editor bundle carry the other three). It is now emitted
         from design/tokens.json `font.reading.stack` like every other
         `--tok-*`, so the ratified order has ONE source and the eventual
         Source-Serif-first cutover is still a one-token edit.

         The token is DECLARED here rather than merely consumed: paper-surface.css
         scopes `--tok-reading-font` to `.bp-paper-surface, .bp-paper-body`, and
         <body> is neither — the reader's surface class rides the <main> inside
         it. Declaring it on the reader body makes `var()` below resolve for
         real instead of silently living on a fallback, and the value is
         byte-identical to the one .bp-paper-surface re-declares, so the
         inner scope is a no-op re-statement rather than an override.
         view_edit_parity_test.exs §10 pins this against the other three. */
      --tok-reading-font: "Iowan Old Style", "Palatino Linotype", Palatino, Charter, Georgia, "Source Serif 4", serif;
      font-family: var(--tok-reading-font);
    }
    @media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {
      body:has(.bp-paper-article),
      body:has(.bp-paper-article) .bp-paper-shell.bp-paper-article {
        --paper-bg:         #0e1614;
        --paper-bg-deep:    #131d19;
        --paper-ink:        #e7ede9;
        --paper-ink-soft:   #9aaaa3;
        --paper-rule:       rgba(231, 237, 233, 0.13);
        --paper-accent:     #45b394;
        /* Warm-dark link border (same value as Studio's html[data-theme=dark]
           block in paper-surface.css) — without it links keep the light-theme
           rgba(162,57,37,0.10) border, invisible on the dark page. */
        --paper-accent-soft: rgba(70, 179, 148, 0.16);
        --paper-reading-accent: #d98a6a; /* S7 stub — S8 consumes */
        /* Callout TONE tokens + faint ink + chrome — the SAME reason as the
           --paper-* above: paper-surface.css keys their DARK values on
           `html[data-theme="dark"]`, which the reader stamps only after its
           pre-paint toggle script runs, so an OS-dark reader (no JS / first
           paint) was left with the LIGHT callout tones (#eff6ff on a #131d19
           page — a light box on the dark article). Re-skin them here,
           byte-mirroring the dark values in paper-surface.css's
           `html[data-theme="dark"] .bp-paper-surface` block. Edit surfaces
           stamp data-theme and get those directly; this is the reader's
           prefers-color-scheme companion. */
        --paper-ink-faint:    #6c7a74;
        --paper-chrome-bg:    #131d19;
        --paper-chrome-border: rgba(231, 237, 233, 0.13);
        --bp-tone-info-bg:    #172032; --bp-tone-info-fg:    #9db8ff;
        --bp-tone-success-bg: #12241c; --bp-tone-success-fg: #5fcf9c;
        --bp-tone-warning-bg: #2a2210; --bp-tone-warning-fg: #e3b661;
        --bp-tone-danger-bg:  #2a1614; --bp-tone-danger-fg:  #f08a80;
        --bp-tone-neutral-bg: #23211e; --bp-tone-neutral-fg: #c9c2b6;
      }
    }
    /* Mail-client popup chrome (color.mailChrome) — the --mail-* window skin
       for the Email view, relocated here (from #bp-mailapp) so the reader
       palette + the mail chrome are single-sourced in ONE marker region; the
       #bp-mailapp consumers stay below. Theme-aware like a real dark mail
       client showing a light HTML email; the served email bytes never change
       with the theme. */
    #bp-mailapp {
      --mail-paper: #ffffff; --mail-bar: #f6faf9; --mail-rule: #dde7e2;
      --mail-ink: #15211d; --mail-soft: #55635e; --mail-accent: #1e5347;
    }
    @media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {
      #bp-mailapp {
        --mail-paper: #131d19; --mail-bar: #0e1614; --mail-rule: #26332d;
        --mail-ink: #e7ede9; --mail-soft: #9aaaa3; --mail-accent: #45b394;
      }
    }
    /* Explicit mode stamp — html[data-theme] companions to the light
       fallback + prefers-color-scheme pair above. The reader's pre-paint
       <head> script stamps data-theme from localStorage `barkpark_theme`
       (shared with Studio) or the OS scheme, and the dark/light toggle
       pill rewrites it — one attribute of extra specificity, so a toggled
       choice beats the @media blocks in BOTH directions. The @media pair
       stays byte-complete as the no-JS / first-paint fallback (it is also
       what the reader-dark-parity guard scans for coverage). */
    html[data-theme="light"] body:has(.bp-paper-article),
    html[data-theme="light"] body:has(.bp-paper-article) .bp-paper-shell.bp-paper-article {
      --paper-bg:         #f6faf9;
      --paper-bg-deep:    #eaf1ee;
      --paper-ink:        #15211d;
      --paper-ink-soft:   #55635e;
      --paper-rule:       #dde7e2;
      --paper-accent:     #1e5347;
      --paper-accent-soft: rgba(30, 83, 71, 0.10);
      --paper-reading-accent: #a23925; /* S7 stub — S8 consumes */
      /* Light re-declarations for every token the @media dark block above
         re-skins — its selector outranks the bare paper-surface light
         fallbacks, so WITHOUT these a forced-light reader under an OS-dark
         scheme keeps dark callout tones / faint ink / chrome (the mirror
         image of the #1217 bug). Values match the un-stamped light path. */
      --paper-ink-faint:    #82918b;
      --paper-chrome-bg:    #f6faf9;
      --paper-chrome-border: rgba(21, 33, 29, 0.12);
      --bp-tone-info-bg:    #e9eff7; --bp-tone-info-fg:    #2d5e8f;
      --bp-tone-success-bg: #e7f2ec; --bp-tone-success-fg: #1e6b52;
      --bp-tone-warning-bg: #f7f0df; --bp-tone-warning-fg: #8a6420;
      --bp-tone-danger-bg:  #f7e9e6; --bp-tone-danger-fg:  #a63a2e;
      --bp-tone-neutral-bg: #edf0ee; --bp-tone-neutral-fg: #4a544f;
    }
    html[data-theme="dark"] body:has(.bp-paper-article),
    html[data-theme="dark"] body:has(.bp-paper-article) .bp-paper-shell.bp-paper-article {
      --paper-bg:         #0e1614;
      --paper-bg-deep:    #131d19;
      --paper-ink:        #e7ede9;
      --paper-ink-soft:   #9aaaa3;
      --paper-rule:       rgba(231, 237, 233, 0.13);
      --paper-accent:     #45b394;
      --paper-accent-soft: rgba(70, 179, 148, 0.16);
      --paper-reading-accent: #d98a6a; /* S7 stub — S8 consumes */
      --paper-ink-faint:    #6c7a74;
      --paper-chrome-bg:    #131d19;
      --paper-chrome-border: rgba(231, 237, 233, 0.13);
      --bp-tone-info-bg:    #172032; --bp-tone-info-fg:    #9db8ff;
      --bp-tone-success-bg: #12241c; --bp-tone-success-fg: #5fcf9c;
      --bp-tone-warning-bg: #2a2210; --bp-tone-warning-fg: #e3b661;
      --bp-tone-danger-bg:  #2a1614; --bp-tone-danger-fg:  #f08a80;
      --bp-tone-neutral-bg: #23211e; --bp-tone-neutral-fg: #c9c2b6;
    }
    html[data-theme="light"] #bp-mailapp {
      --mail-paper: #ffffff; --mail-bar: #f6faf9; --mail-rule: #dde7e2;
      --mail-ink: #15211d; --mail-soft: #55635e; --mail-accent: #1e5347;
    }
    html[data-theme="dark"] #bp-mailapp {
      --mail-paper: #131d19; --mail-bar: #0e1614; --mail-rule: #26332d;
      --mail-ink: #e7ede9; --mail-soft: #9aaaa3; --mail-accent: #45b394;
    }
    /* PRINT: the LIGHT palette, re-stamped from the same token data as the
       light blocks above — paged output is ink on white whatever the screen
       scheme or the stamped [data-theme] was. See printRestamp in
       design/emit.mjs; the white ground + chrome hiding stay in the hand
       region's @media print block further down this sheet. */
    @media print {
      html body:has(.bp-paper-article),
      html body:has(.bp-paper-article) .bp-paper-shell.bp-paper-article,
      html[data-theme] body:has(.bp-paper-article),
      html[data-theme] body:has(.bp-paper-article) .bp-paper-shell.bp-paper-article {
        --paper-bg:         #f6faf9;
        --paper-bg-deep:    #eaf1ee;
        --paper-ink:        #15211d;
        --paper-ink-soft:   #55635e;
        --paper-rule:       #dde7e2;
        --paper-accent:     #1e5347;
        --paper-accent-soft: rgba(30, 83, 71, 0.10);
        --paper-reading-accent: #a23925; /* S7 stub — S8 consumes */
        --paper-ink-faint:    #82918b;
        --paper-chrome-bg:    #f6faf9;
        --paper-chrome-border: rgba(21, 33, 29, 0.12);
        --bp-tone-info-bg:    #e9eff7; --bp-tone-info-fg:    #2d5e8f;
        --bp-tone-success-bg: #e7f2ec; --bp-tone-success-fg: #1e6b52;
        --bp-tone-warning-bg: #f7f0df; --bp-tone-warning-fg: #8a6420;
        --bp-tone-danger-bg:  #f7e9e6; --bp-tone-danger-fg:  #a63a2e;
        --bp-tone-neutral-bg: #edf0ee; --bp-tone-neutral-fg: #4a544f;
      }
    }
    /* ── theme identity (data-bp-theme) — orthogonal to light/dark mode; the bare declarations above are the evergreen fallback (no attribute → renders exactly as today) ── */
    html[data-bp-theme="evergreen"] body:has(.bp-paper-article),
    html[data-bp-theme="evergreen"] body:has(.bp-paper-article) .bp-paper-shell.bp-paper-article {
      --paper-bg: #f6faf9;
      --paper-bg-deep: #eaf1ee;
      --paper-ink: #15211d;
      --paper-ink-soft: #55635e;
      --paper-rule: #dde7e2;
      --paper-accent: #1e5347;
      --paper-accent-soft: rgba(30, 83, 71, 0.10);
      --paper-reading-accent: #a23925;
    }
    @media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {
      html[data-bp-theme="evergreen"] body:has(.bp-paper-article),
      html[data-bp-theme="evergreen"] body:has(.bp-paper-article) .bp-paper-shell.bp-paper-article {
        --paper-bg: #0e1614;
        --paper-bg-deep: #131d19;
        --paper-ink: #e7ede9;
        --paper-ink-soft: #9aaaa3;
        --paper-rule: rgba(231, 237, 233, 0.13);
        --paper-accent: #45b394;
        --paper-accent-soft: rgba(70, 179, 148, 0.16);
        --paper-reading-accent: #d98a6a;
        --paper-ink-faint: #6c7a74;
        --paper-chrome-bg: #131d19;
        --paper-chrome-border: rgba(231, 237, 233, 0.13);
        --bp-tone-info-bg: #172032; --bp-tone-info-fg: #9db8ff;
        --bp-tone-success-bg: #12241c; --bp-tone-success-fg: #5fcf9c;
        --bp-tone-warning-bg: #2a2210; --bp-tone-warning-fg: #e3b661;
        --bp-tone-danger-bg: #2a1614; --bp-tone-danger-fg: #f08a80;
        --bp-tone-neutral-bg: #23211e; --bp-tone-neutral-fg: #c9c2b6;
      }
    }
    html[data-bp-theme="evergreen"] #bp-mailapp {
      --mail-paper: #ffffff; --mail-bar: #f6faf9; --mail-rule: #dde7e2;
      --mail-ink: #15211d; --mail-soft: #55635e; --mail-accent: #1e5347;
    }
    @media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {
      html[data-bp-theme="evergreen"] #bp-mailapp {
        --mail-paper: #131d19; --mail-bar: #0e1614; --mail-rule: #26332d;
        --mail-ink: #e7ede9; --mail-soft: #9aaaa3; --mail-accent: #45b394;
      }
    }
    html[data-bp-theme="evergreen"][data-theme="light"] body:has(.bp-paper-article),
    html[data-bp-theme="evergreen"][data-theme="light"] body:has(.bp-paper-article) .bp-paper-shell.bp-paper-article {
      --paper-bg: #f6faf9;
      --paper-bg-deep: #eaf1ee;
      --paper-ink: #15211d;
      --paper-ink-soft: #55635e;
      --paper-rule: #dde7e2;
      --paper-accent: #1e5347;
      --paper-accent-soft: rgba(30, 83, 71, 0.10);
      --paper-reading-accent: #a23925;
      --paper-ink-faint: #82918b;
      --paper-chrome-bg: #f6faf9;
      --paper-chrome-border: rgba(21, 33, 29, 0.12);
      --bp-tone-info-bg: #e9eff7; --bp-tone-info-fg: #2d5e8f;
      --bp-tone-success-bg: #e7f2ec; --bp-tone-success-fg: #1e6b52;
      --bp-tone-warning-bg: #f7f0df; --bp-tone-warning-fg: #8a6420;
      --bp-tone-danger-bg: #f7e9e6; --bp-tone-danger-fg: #a63a2e;
      --bp-tone-neutral-bg: #edf0ee; --bp-tone-neutral-fg: #4a544f;
    }
    html[data-bp-theme="evergreen"][data-theme="dark"] body:has(.bp-paper-article),
    html[data-bp-theme="evergreen"][data-theme="dark"] body:has(.bp-paper-article) .bp-paper-shell.bp-paper-article {
      --paper-bg: #0e1614;
      --paper-bg-deep: #131d19;
      --paper-ink: #e7ede9;
      --paper-ink-soft: #9aaaa3;
      --paper-rule: rgba(231, 237, 233, 0.13);
      --paper-accent: #45b394;
      --paper-accent-soft: rgba(70, 179, 148, 0.16);
      --paper-reading-accent: #d98a6a;
      --paper-ink-faint: #6c7a74;
      --paper-chrome-bg: #131d19;
      --paper-chrome-border: rgba(231, 237, 233, 0.13);
      --bp-tone-info-bg: #172032; --bp-tone-info-fg: #9db8ff;
      --bp-tone-success-bg: #12241c; --bp-tone-success-fg: #5fcf9c;
      --bp-tone-warning-bg: #2a2210; --bp-tone-warning-fg: #e3b661;
      --bp-tone-danger-bg: #2a1614; --bp-tone-danger-fg: #f08a80;
      --bp-tone-neutral-bg: #23211e; --bp-tone-neutral-fg: #c9c2b6;
    }
    html[data-bp-theme="evergreen"][data-theme="light"] #bp-mailapp {
      --mail-paper: #ffffff; --mail-bar: #f6faf9; --mail-rule: #dde7e2;
      --mail-ink: #15211d; --mail-soft: #55635e; --mail-accent: #1e5347;
    }
    html[data-bp-theme="evergreen"][data-theme="dark"] #bp-mailapp {
      --mail-paper: #131d19; --mail-bar: #0e1614; --mail-rule: #26332d;
      --mail-ink: #e7ede9; --mail-soft: #9aaaa3; --mail-accent: #45b394;
    }
    /* PRINT: the LIGHT palette, re-stamped from the same token data as the
       light blocks above — paged output is ink on white whatever the screen
       scheme or the stamped [data-theme] was. See printRestamp in
       design/emit.mjs; the white ground + chrome hiding stay in the hand
       region's @media print block further down this sheet. */
    @media print {
      html[data-bp-theme="evergreen"] body:has(.bp-paper-article),
      html[data-bp-theme="evergreen"] body:has(.bp-paper-article) .bp-paper-shell.bp-paper-article,
